warmed through the OS page cache at any length, holding no memory (191 → 4 ms worst first-play on a 124-minute channel), so nothing starves and nothing waits; opening the editor holds a background render at trickle until the preview is on screen.
- Export: three paths in order — instant packet copy (unedited, and SINCE J6 only over the RAW channel, because there is no composite file any more: screen-only when the step matches the take's geometry incl. `max`, 452 KB instant at 3024x1964. A take WITH A CAMERA has nothing to copy and takes the render, pre-made at stop by F16b — 0.3 ms at the press, measured) · smart cut (trims/cuts; re-encode only [cut → next keyframe]) · render (worker, 5-6× realtime, decode-bound); prerender makes the file before it is asked for (AT STOP for a take whose export must render, AND FROM AN EDIT since J5 2026-09-04, robert (27) — `core/compose/editRender.ts` is the one door: opening a take starts nothing, an undo back to it cancels, a packet copy starts nothing, a real edit starts one 1.2 s after it settles; paced by pressure, first load shed) — and since J1 an edit no longer throws that work away: the render is chunked into content-named files on a grid that is also the file's keyframe cadence (2.5 s since robert (28) 2026-09-04, `?gop=`; it was 5 s) and concatenated by packet copy, so an edit costs only the chunks whose pixels moved (2 of 180 on a 15-minute take), an undo costs nothing, and a killed tab resumes at the last complete chunk; SINCE J9 2026-09-05 a piece LOST mid-export costs the piece and not the take — a chunk that vanishes between the missing-set and the concatenation used to come out as OPFS's own NotFoundError (not a ChunkedRenderUnavailable, so the press FAILED after the whole render), and every other refusal fell back to an unbroken render from frame zero, which on a 90-minute max60 take is a second ~81-minute generation. `renderChunkedResuming` is what callers use: a RECOVERABLE failure re-enters once and renders only what is gone (proved on the encoder, `exp chunkrender` lane j9 — pre-J9 FAILED, now 1 of 12 chunks re-rendered, 900 packets of 900). A live export leaves a claim (`rclaim-<heartbeat>`) and the boot sweep stands down while one is fresh, because App.tsx sweeps with NO keep set — a second tab could delete the render in front of him. And the cache is now ASKED whether the take fits: the render measures its own bytes/chunk, evicts other takes' chunks for room, and when the take is bigger than the cap (a 90-min max60 take is ~6.8 GB against a quarter of the quota) says so in the first seconds and renders unbroken ONCE. J10 2026-09-05 gave the PRE-RENDER the same treatment (it was the same defect one file over): `sweepPrerenderBlobs` kept only the blob claimed by THIS page's in-memory `job`, so any second boot deleted a finished pre-render another tab was about to serve — the blob key now names its session and a session holding one beats `pclaim-<stamp>-<session>`; a key written before J10 has no session and is swept as it was. J10 also closed a hole that would have silently undone J9: `reclaim.ts` isSweepable allows anything not on its list, so both claim prefixes were reclaimable as orphans AT BOOT, beside the sweeps that read them — both are now excluded, and both self-expire in two minutes. Verified on prod through the real boot sweep: live sibling's pre-render KEPT, dead session's swept, pre-J10 key swept, stale claim swept, live claim kept. Storage is also asked to be durable (App.tsx, J9): everything was best-effort, i.e. the browser may evict an unexported take on a full disk; it does NOT raise the quota and Chrome never prompts for it; exports are persisted background jobs in a dock on every screen, parallel, and RESUMABLE since J1 — a restarted job finds the chunks the dead one finished; size panel = model + probe (the probe now picks its ten seconds from the take's own activity profile rather than taking the middle — text 0.91-0.96×, motion 0.94-1.03× of the file it delivers, B1b 2026-09-04); certificate JSON in every file's comment tag; For-AI PDF export (AI1) in a worker.
